# One module, one translation unit (panel 093; M-separate-compilation
# step 6). The fused path in emit.hero stays the artifact path — the
# seed, --emit-c, the blessed emissions and the fixpoint all read it —
# and THIS is the build path: `heroes build` emits one .c per module,
# each compiled to its own cached object and linked.
#
# The v1 trade, taken deliberately and priced by panel 093's engineer:
# a monomorphised instance and a per-type function are DUPLICATED per
# TU under HERO_TU_LOCAL, which the per-module prelude defines as
# `__attribute__((unused)) static` — the probes' own precedent — so an
# unused copy is silent instead of `-Wunused-function` against §7's
# zero-warning bar.
#
# What the first draft got wrong, and the measurement that corrected it:
# the whole-program sections were emitted WHOLE in every TU, on the
# argument that a shared section is byte-identical by construction and
# needs no dedup machinery. It is byte-identical, and it is also 87% of
# a TU that has no use for it — one TU of this 157-module program was
# 19,193 lines, and the build went 188 s -> 484 s. So typedefs, per-type
# functions and descriptors are all filtered by ONE per-TU reach
# (`emit.reach_of` over the TU's own members, read through
# `emit_synth.Wanted`). One filter over all four sections is what makes
# them agree: trimming the descriptors alone left `h_parse_Expr_desc`
# undeclared under satellites that still mentioned it.
#
# The one whole-program section that stays whole is the PROTOTYPE
# block, and that is R3 rather than an oversight: a cross-TU call
# resolves against it, and the declaring TU compiles the same text
# beside its own definition so a divergence is clang's error there.
#
# WHAT A TU CONTAINS, in the fused unit's own order: prelude (runtime
# include, THIS module's extern headers and asserts and probes, the
# TU-local macro, this TU's strings) · the whole-program typedefs · the
# per-type prototypes (macro'd) · this TU's descriptors · prototypes
# for every ordinary function in the program (cross-TU calls resolve
# against these — and the declaring TU compiles the same text beside
# its own definitions, so a divergence is clang's `conflicting types`
# in the declaring TU, panel 093 R3's measured repair) · this module's
# signatures and bodies, plus the instances it uses, static · the
# per-type bodies (macro'd) · the shim, in the root TU only.
#
# WHO IS IN A TU: the module's own functions (by their declaration's
# source position), plus every monomorphised instance reachable from
# them through calls — transitively, because an instance may call
# another instance. An instance's own home is the template's module,
# which is exactly why it cannot live there (panel 093 R2: the cache
# arrow — a defining TU whose text depended on its dependents' calls
# would go stale under a key that cannot see them).

use ast
use source
use check_state
use ir_state
use emit
use emit_synth
use emit_members
use emit_writer
use emit_decls
use emit_typeorder
use emit_perfn
use emit_descriptors
use emit_signature
use emit_body

# One module's translation unit. The caller has already run the gate
# over the whole program (a refusal is program-wide) and monomorphised;
# `is_root` says whether this TU carries the shim.
function unit(module: str, p: ir_state.Program, c: check_state.Checked, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl], version: str, is_root: bool, whole: emit_members.Whole) -> str
    mine = emit_members.members(module, p, c, s, decls, shown: whole.shown, by_decl: whole.by_decl)
    keep = mine.defined
    tu_p = emit_members.tu_program(p, keep: keep)
    # THIS TU's reach, and every whole-program section is filtered by
    # it — typedefs, per-type functions, descriptors. Trimming the
    # descriptors alone left `h_parse_Expr_desc` undeclared under
    # satellites that still mentioned it; trimming NOTHING made a TU
    # 19,193 lines where 87% belonged to other modules, and the
    # compiler's own build went 188 s -> 484 s (measured 2026-08-25).
    # One filter over all four sections is what makes them agree.
    walked = emit.reach_of(tu_p, c, target: .program_build, shown: emit_members.all_of(tu_p))
    # A PROTOTYPE names types too, and the body walk above cannot see
    # the ones belonging to another TU's function. So the signatures of
    # everything this TU declares join the reach before the filter is
    # built — asked of the value in hand (these exact functions) rather
    # than argued from "an argument must be a local anyway", which is
    # true today and is a premise about the world (CLAUDE.md §11).
    reached: {i64: bool} @ walked.reached
    for at in mine.needed
        f = p.functions[at]
        reached[f.result] @ true
        for slot in f.params
            reached[f.slots[slot].ty] @ true
    # ONE set feeds all four sections, and that is the invariant rather
    # than a tidiness. Every time a section got its own answer the TU
    # stopped compiling: descriptors from the whole program against
    # per-TU typedefs (`h_token_Span`), prototypes for the whole program
    # against per-TU typedefs (`h_source_InputFile`), a wanted record
    # whose field's `_hash` nobody declared (`h_diag_Kind_hash`), and a
    # wanted `T?` whose payload descriptor nobody defined
    # (`h_clitoolchain_Toolchain_desc`). Four symptoms, one cause, and
    # the cause is two sets where there should be one.
    declared = emit_members.declared_reach(c, decls, seed: emit.closed_over(c, reached: reached))
    want = emit_synth.wanted_of(c, reached: declared)
    names = whole.names
    w: emit_writer.Writer @ emit_writer.new_writer(module)
    emit_decls.prelude(@w, tu_p, target: .program_build, c: c, names: names, s: s, decls: decls, version: version, tu_local: "__attribute__((unused)) static")
    emit_typeorder.definitions(@w, c, names, s, decls, want: want)
    emit_perfn.prototypes(@w, c, names, decls, want: want)
    # `declared`, not `walked.reached`: an option this TU prints `_hash`
    # for takes its payload's descriptor address, so the descriptor set
    # has to read the same reach the printer does. `constructed` stays
    # the NARROW one on purpose — it is the container narrowing that
    # keeps a `[Probe]` nobody builds from getting a descriptor nothing
    # points at (-Wunused-const-variable, §7's zero-warning bar).
    emit_descriptors.definitions(@w, c, names, decls, reachable: declared, constructed: walked.constructed, want: want)
    # What this TU defines and what it calls, in one ascending walk:
    # a cross-TU call resolves against the callee's prototype, and the
    # DECLARING TU compiles that same text beside its own definition,
    # so the two cannot drift apart without clang saying `conflicting
    # types` there (panel 093 R3). An instance's prototype rides the
    # same list and carries HERO_TU_LOCAL from `signature` itself,
    # which is right: its definition is in this TU too.
    for at in mine.needed
        f = p.functions[at]
        if emit_decls.emitted(f, target: .program_build)
            emit_signature.prototype(@w, f, c, names, s, decls)
    for at in keep
        f = p.functions[at]
        if emit_decls.emitted(f, target: .program_build)
            # The FULL program, not the TU's slice: a call names its
            # callee by searching p.functions, and a cross-TU call's
            # callee is exactly the function this TU does not define.
            # Passing the slice here turned `eval.calculate(...)` into
            # `hero_unreachable()` at exit 0 potential — found by the
            # first real per-module build, as three symptoms of this
            # one cause.
            emit_body.definition(@w, p, c, names, s, decls, f: f)
    emit_perfn.bodies(@w, c, names, s, decls, want: want)
    if is_root
        entry = emit.entry_point(p)
        if !entry.is_err()
            emit_decls.shim(@w, p.functions[entry.must()], s)
    return emit_writer.finish(w)
